Section 42: Coming into force
(1) The following provisions come into force on the day on which this Act receives Royal Assent—
(1)(a) Part 1 (section 1);
(1)(b) in Part 3—
(1)(b)(i) section 10, but that section has effect in accordance with section 10(4);
(1)(b)(ii) section 11, but that section has effect in accordance with section 11(2);
(1)(b)(iii) section 27, but subsections (2)(d), (3) and (4) of that section have effect in accordance with section 27(5);
(1)(c) Part 4 (sections 29 to 35 and Schedule 3), but it only has effect for the purposes of a Senedd election at which the poll is held on or after 5 April 2021;
(1)(d) in Part 5, sections 37 and 38;
(1)(e) this Part (sections 39 to 43).
(2) Part 2 (sections 2 to 9 and Schedule 1) comes into force on 6 May 2020.
(3) In Part 3—
(3)(a) sections 12 to 26 come into force on 1 June 2020;
(3)(b) section 28 and Schedule 2 come into force on a day appointed by the Welsh Ministers in an order made by statutory instrument.
(4) An order under subsection (3)(b) may include transitional, transitory or saving provision.
(5) In Part 5, section 36 comes into force on the day of the first Senedd election at which the poll is held on or after 5 April 2021.

Section 24: Protection of information about persons aged under 16
(1) A registration officer must not publish, supply or otherwise disclose a young person’s information, except in accordance with—
(1)(a) section 25, or
(1)(b) regulations under section 26.
(2) In this section and sections 25 and 26— “a young person’s information” (“ gwybodaeth person ifanc ”) means any entry in— “absent voters record or list” (“ cofnod neu restr o bleidleiswyr absennol ”) means any of the following— “registration officer” (“ swyddog cofrestru ”) includes—
(2)(a) the register of local government electors, or
(2)(b) an absent voters record or list,
(2)(c) relating to a person who, at the time of the publication, supply or other disclosure, is under the age of 16, and “young person” is to be interpreted accordingly;
(2)(a) a record kept under article 8(3), 9(6) or 12(6) of the 2007 Order;
(2)(b) a list kept under article 10 or 12(8) of the 2007 Order;
(2)(f) “register of local government electors” (“ cofrestr o etholwyr llywodraeth leol ”) includes any notice of an alteration in the register under section 13A(2), 13AB(2) or 13B(3), (3B) or (3D) of the 1983 Act;
(2)(a) a deputy of a registration officer;
(2)(b) a person appointed to assist a registration officer in the carrying out of the registration officer’s functions;
(2)(c) a person, in the course of the person’s employment, assisting a registration officer in the carrying out of those functions.

Section 1: Power to construct works
(1) The authorised undertaker may construct and maintain the scheduled works or any part of them, as specified in schedule 1.
(2) Subject to section 2 (Power to deviate), the scheduled works may only be constructed in the lines or situations shown on the Parliamentary plans and in accordance with the levels shown on the Parliamentary sections.
(3) Subject to subsection (7), the authorised undertaker may carry out and maintain such of the following works as may be necessary or expedient for the purposes of, in connection with or in consequence of, the scheduled works, namely—
(3)(a) stopping places;
(3)(b) works required for, or in connection with, the control of any vehicular and pedestrian traffic on the authorised tramway;
(3)(c) works required for the strengthening, improvement, repair or reconstruction of any road;
(3)(d) works for the strengthening, alteration or demolition of any building or structure;
(3)(e) works to alter the position of any road furniture or apparatus, including mains, sewers, drains and cables and lights;
(3)(f) works to alter the course of, or otherwise interfere with, rivers, streams or watercourses;
(3)(g) landscaping and other works to mitigate any adverse effects of the construction, maintenance or operation of the authorised works; and
(3)(h) facilities and works for the benefit or protection of land or premises affected by the authorised works.
(4) Subject to subsection (7), the authorised undertaker may carry out and maintain such other works (of whatever nature) as may be necessary or expedient for the purposes of, in connection with or in consequence of, the construction of the scheduled works.
(5) The authorised undertaker may remove any works constructed by it pursuant to this Act which have been constructed as temporary works or which it no longer requires.
(6) Where the authorised undertaker lays down conduits for the accommodation of cables or other apparatus for the purposes of the authorised works or associated traffic control, it may provide in, or in connection with, such conduits accommodation for the apparatus of any other person, and manholes and other facilities for access to such accommodation, and may permit the use of such conduits and facilities on such terms and conditions as may be agreed between it and such other person.
(7) Subsections (3) and (4) shall only authorise the carrying out or maintenance of works—
(7)(a) within the limits of deviation shown on the Parliamentary plans for the scheduled works; or
(7)(b) on land specified in columns (1) and (2) of Parts 1 and 2 of schedule 6 for the purpose specified in relation to that land in column (3) of those Parts of that schedule (being land shown on the Parliamentary plans as lying within the limits of land to be acquired or used).

Section 26: Socially responsible procurement duty: outsourcing services contracts
(1) The particular actions mentioned in section 24(5)(c) are—
(1)(a) having regard to the public services outsourcing and workforce code published by the Welsh Ministers under section 32;
(1)(b) in designing and carrying out procedures preceding the award of the outsourcing services contract, considering whether the contract should include social public workforce clauses;
(1)(c) in negotiating and awarding the contract, taking all reasonable steps—
(1)(c)(i) to include any social public workforce clauses it considers should be included;
(1)(c)(ii) to ensure that clauses included in the contract can be implemented;
(1)(d) in managing the contract, taking all reasonable steps to ensure that any social public workforce clauses included in the contract are implemented;
(1)(e) (see sections 32 to 37 for further provision about the public services outsourcing and workforce code, the meaning of “social public workforce clauses” and their application to outsourcing services contracts).
(2) In this Part, “ an outsourcing services contract ” means a contract under which—
(2)(a) a requirement to provide a public service provided by, or previously provided by, a contracting authority is transferred to another person, or
(2)(b) another person agrees to undertake any other function undertaken by, or previously undertaken by, a contracting authority;
(2)(c) and “ outsourced ” is to be construed accordingly.
